A global event monitor is a token that never fires, not a missing method.

+[NSEvent addGlobalMonitorForEventsMatchingMask:handler:] did not exist, so asking for one RAISED:

  NSApplication got exception: +[NSEvent addGlobalMonitorForEventsMatchingMask:handler:]:
  unrecognized selector sent to instance 0x75eceb23a930

AppKit catches exceptions per event, so the raise did not stop the application; it abandoned
whatever the caller was part way through and said nothing. Qt asks for one while bringing up a
dialog, and CMake raises it opening its generator wizard (#234).

A GLOBAL MONITOR WATCHES OTHER APPLICATIONS EVENTS, and under this port there are none, so a token
that never fires is the truthful answer. Returning nil would also be defensible, since macOS returns
nil when the process is not trusted for accessibility, but that sends a caller down its
permission-denied path for a reason that is not true here.

removeMonitor: is now a quiet no-op rather than NSUnimplementedMethod(). A caller balancing an add
it never used is not doing anything wrong and should not be reported as a defect, and with the
global monitor now handed out the balancing call happens on a normal path.

addLocalMonitorForEventsMatchingMask: is left as it was. It is a REAL feature, observing this
application own events before they are dispatched, and answering nil is already the documented
outcome for a monitor that matches nothing. Implementing it properly is separate work.

MEASURED: the unrecognized selector is gone and the run now has ZERO caught exceptions, where
before it had one.

HONEST LIMIT: this does NOT fix the CMake crash it was found through. That crash survives
unchanged, still a null dereference at address 0x0 with the same wizard symbols on the stack, and
#234 stays open. This is a real gap closed on the way past, not the cause.

--- a/AppKit/NSEvent.subproj/NSEvent.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSEvent.subproj/NSEvent.m
@@ -389,8 +389,26 @@
     return nil;
 }
 
+/*
+ * A GLOBAL MONITOR WATCHES OTHER APPLICATIONS EVENTS, and there are none here, so the honest
+ * answer is a token that never fires rather than no method at all.
+ *
+ * ABSENT, THIS RAISED. Qt asks for one while bringing up a dialog, AppKit catches the exception per
+ * event, and the setup around it is abandoned half done; CMake then crashed with a null dereference
+ * opening its generator wizard. Task #234.
+ *
+ * Returning nil would also be defensible, since macOS returns nil when the process is not trusted
+ * for accessibility, but that sends a caller down its permission-denied path for a reason that is
+ * not true here.
+ */
++ (id) addGlobalMonitorForEventsMatchingMask: (NSEventMask) mask
+                                     handler: (void (^)(NSEvent *event)) block
+{
+    return [[[NSObject alloc] init] autorelease];
+}
+
+/* Quietly, because a caller balancing an add it never used should not be reported as a defect. */
 + (void) removeMonitor: (id) eventMonitor {
-    NSUnimplementedMethod();
 }
 
 
